
// draft
always_reversible(iterable: Int[]) -> Int[]
	note "yield the iterable's items in reverse order, whether or not the iterable supports reversed(); caching versus calling reversed() is execution, not part of the relation"
	// Int stands in for any element type; the relation does not depend on what the items are
	# The output has the same elements as the input, each occurring as many times as it does in the
	# input.

	# Of two input elements with different values, the one that comes earlier in the input comes
	# later in the output.

	# The output's first element is the input's last element.

	# The output after its first element is the reversal of the input without its last element.
	// example wanted: an empty iterable: there is no last element to put first, and nothing is
	// cached or yielded
	// example wanted: a single item: its reversal is the same one-item sequence
	// example wanted: the docstring's generator over range(3), items [0, 1, 2]: an iterable that is
	// not Reversible, so the remaining items are cached and yielded back to front
	// example wanted: items [1, 2, 1]: equal values at both ends, where the different-values order
	// claim says nothing and the first-element and rest-of-output claims decide the positions
	// example wanted: items [3, 3]: every value equal, so only the element-count claim
	// distinguishes outputs
